Camera source and playback request identity
===========================================

HLS reuse now requires the same camera, organization scope, quality, source codec,
live/recorded mode, authenticated source URL and optional caller request key.
An explicit key cannot override those boundaries. Identical requests still share
one owned child. The stored key is a namespaced HMAC using the application secret;
it does not retain raw URL credentials or caller text in session/cache keys.

Recorded request identity includes the full ISO start time (including fractional
seconds and offset) and duration. Equivalent instants expressed with different
offsets may create separate sessions; requests for distinct instants cannot join
because second-only formatting discarded an offset or fractional component.

The recorded probe cache uses organization, camera, authenticated source and exact
recording request identity. A negative result for one window cannot block a
separate window. Quality-only changes reuse probe metadata while using their own
encoding session. Inconclusive probes are never cached. The existing 30-minute
TTL remains; expired entries are removed on access and the process-local cache is
bounded to 256 entries, evicting oldest inserted results. An exact cached window
can still remain stale within that TTL. No distributed cache or single-flight
probe deduplication is claimed.

Playback URL-build failures log only the camera identifier and a fixed category,
not native adapter exception text that may contain credentials.

Current permission checks remain required before start and continuation. This
change does not distribute sessions between API workers or hosts, add viewer
leases, change finite-recording completion behavior, fence device writes, or
qualify vendor hardware. Local synthetic media tests are not RTSP acceptance.
